
Configure git with IDE: (I am using Visual Studio Community) 

On your computer or labtop.
Install Git using CLI:
winget search git
winget install Git.Git

Install Visual Studio:
winget search "Visual Studio"
winget install Microsoft.VisualStudio.2022.Community 
Or
winget install Microsoft.VisualStudioCode 

On your computer or labtop.
Change Environment Variable: Click Windows Icon and search Environment variable.  
Find path for IDE: Right click on icon-> Properties -> Target  
Target: "C:\Program Files\Microsoft Visual Studio\18\Community\Common7\IDE\devenv.exe"  
Add to user path: C:\Program Files\Microsoft Visual Studio\18\Community\Common7\IDE\
Tell git to go to your IDE path and open that IDE or text editor.

Open Git-Bash.
If your using VS code: git config --global core.editor "code --wait  --new-window" 
If your using VS Community: 
git config --global core.editor "cmd.exe //C start //wait \"\" 
\"C:\Program Files\Microsoft Visual Studio\18\Community\Common7\IDE\devenv.exe\" /Edit"

Open Git-Bash
Understand the config subcommand.
axruf@axruffin MINGW64 ~
$ git config --help 
git config --global user.name "My name here" : Change user name. 
git config --global user.email "example.biz@yahoo.com" : Change user email 
git config --edit --global : Show global settings.
git init : Initialize a git local repo. 
git clone : Download a project from remote.     
git add : Prepare a file to staging.
git commit : Commit a file to the repo. 


